{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 22, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187 and one of the most prominent nobles in the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the twelfth century. He was born in 1140 the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His mother was the daughter of King Baldwin II which gave him strong royal connections. Raymond inherited the county at a young age and his early rule was marked by regents due to his youth. He was a key player in the politics of the Crusader states and often found himself at the center of power struggles. His lineage and marriage alliances made him a central figure in the fragile alliances between the Latin states of the Levant.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III was known for his cautious and diplomatic approach to governance. He often opposed the more aggressive policies of the kings of Jerusalem especially those of Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy. Raymond was a rival of the powerful military orders and was frequently at odds with the Templars and the Hospitallers. He was accused of treason multiple times for his dealings with Muslim leaders including Saladin. These accusations were often politically motivated and stemmed from his desire to avoid unnecessary wars that could weaken the county. Raymond preferred negotiation over confrontation and believed that diplomacy could preserve Christian holdings in the region. His stance made him unpopular among many of his contemporaries who favored bold military action.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond III played a critical role in the events leading to the Battle of Hattin in 1187. He was one of the few nobles who warned against the rash decision to march to Tiberias and confront Saladin directly. He argued that the army was vulnerable to ambush in the arid terrain and that the water supply would be cut off. His warnings were ignored and the disastrous defeat at Hattin followed. After the battle Raymond was one of the few high ranking nobles to escape and he returned to Tripoli. He died in 1187 shortly after the fall of the city of Tripoli to Saladin. His death marked the end of a long and controversial reign that reflected the complex realities of Crusader politics. Raymond III remains a figure of debate among historians who see him as either a pragmatic leader or a traitor to the Crusading cause. His legacy is tied to the decline of the Crusader states in the Levant.", "logprobs": [{"token": "*", "logprob": -3.111314072157256e-05}, {"token": "highlight", "logprob": -0.0024852838832885027}, {"token": "ed", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " section", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " part", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "*", "logprob": -3.71926071238704e-05}, {"token": "  \n", "logprob": -0.03804221749305725}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -9.417489309271332e-06}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.008450583554804325}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.014097276143729687}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.09045735746622086}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -1.0609570381348021e-05}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0009839460253715515}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.00025376438861712813}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -3.135155202471651e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.311301275563892e-06}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.9740936160087585}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -3.194758028257638e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "8", "logprob": -7.271740287251305e-06}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"toke